## Decision

Adopt a versioned canonical evaluation representation that is independent of both authored gold spellings and scanner rule ids. Gold loaders and scanner adapters must emit the same representation before scoring.

The representation separates:

- observation identity;
- exact semantic concept leaf and taxonomy ancestry;
- component type, subtype, and instance/vendor;
- evidence locations;
- display names.

Display names and finding labels are never identity inputs. In particular, `subject.name` and scanner labels may not rescue a key mismatch.

PII relations such as SSN and driver license under national identifier are taxonomy parent/child relations, not equivalence. The scorer reports exact-leaf correctness separately from ancestor-category correctness. A curated adapter may map a detector rule id to one canonical leaf, but the map is closed, reviewed, versioned, and one-to-one at that leaf. It may not turn `ssn` into an exact match for every national identifier.

Data-item source names such as `uid` are separate from inferred concepts such as user identifier. Component type/subtype/instance are likewise separate fields instead of one overloaded `type:name` string.

New gold and fixtures use the canonical representation. Existing corpus data receives a mechanical migration where unambiguous and explicit adjudication where ambiguous. A temporary versioned legacy loader may support the old keys during migration, with validation and a removal milestone. Fixture Jest and corpus evaluation must pass through the same adapter and contract tests.

Rejected alternatives:

- matcher rule ids as the public vocabulary, because that couples truth to the implementation being measured;
- treating current gold strings as the complete contract, because they mix taxonomy, source fields, instances, and display names;
- open-ended token equivalence groups;
- parent/child concepts treated as exact aliases;
- using labels or human-readable names as hidden identity aliases.

Follow-on implementation must add the canonical schema/IR, taxonomy and adapter-map versions, migration validation, exact and ancestor metrics, shared fixture adapters, and synchronized docs. Detector retuning remains out of scope.
